{% tool-context
   title="Database Sizing Workbench"
   question="What does a database sizing plan need to prove?"
   definition="A database sizing plan must prove that retained data fits on disk and that peak reads, writes, cache misses, and connections stay inside measured node limits."
   inputs="Define row growth, retention, compression, indexes, replicas, hot data, peak workload, provisioned nodes, and tested per-node capacity."
   outcome="Find the first storage or serving bottleneck, preserve explicit headroom, and separate measured limits from illustrative assumptions."
   experiment="Start with the planned peak, then inject retention growth, new indexes, node loss, a hot partition, and compaction pressure." /%}

